{# =================================================================== ABSTRACT #}
<section class="front" id="abstract">
  <h1>Abstract</h1>
  <p><strong>Background.</strong> Machine-learning studies on the public <i>PostPartum Depression</i> questionnaire dataset report accuracies between {{ n.lit_low | f2 }}% and {{ n.lit_high | f1 }}%. They rarely explain individual predictions, quantify uncertainty, test whether one model is really better than another, or examine the fact that most rows of the dataset repeat an earlier answer pattern.</p>
  <p><strong>Objective.</strong> To build and evaluate an explainable stacking ensemble that predicts a self-reported <i>Feeling anxious</i> answer from eight other questionnaire answers and an age band, and to audit how far the reported accuracy reflects prediction on new answer patterns.</p>
  <p><strong>Methods.</strong> All {{ '{:,}'.format(n.rows) }} responses were split into a stratified {{ '{:,}'.format(n.train_n) }}-person training set and a {{ n.test_n }}-person test set. Seven models competed: logistic regression, random forest, XGBoost, LightGBM, CatBoost, a multilayer perceptron, and a proposed Hybrid Stack in which CatBoost, XGBoost, random forest and the perceptron feed a logistic-regression meta-learner through out-of-fold predictions. Models were compared with ten-fold cross-validation, a single final test, the Friedman test, Wilcoxon signed-rank tests with Holm correction, McNemar tests and a bootstrap confidence interval. The Hybrid Stack was explained with SHAP and LIME, which were compared on agreement, deletion-based faithfulness, stability and speed. Calibration, conformal prediction, a counterfactual example and an age-group check were added. Finally, grouped cross-validation kept every answer pattern on one side of each split.</p>
  <p><strong>Results.</strong> The Hybrid Stack reached {{ test[H].accuracy | f2 }}% test accuracy (95% CI {{ n.ci }}%), F1 {{ test[H].f1 | f2 }}% and ROC-AUC {{ test[H].roc_auc | f3 }}. CatBoost reached {{ test['CatBoost'].accuracy | f2 }}% on the same split and {{ cv['CatBoost'].mean | f2 }}% &plusmn; {{ cv['CatBoost'].std | f2 }} in cross-validation. The Hybrid Stack was significantly better than logistic regression (Holm-corrected p = {{ wx_lr.p_holm | f3 }}) and not significantly different from the other five models. SHAP and LIME both ranked <i>{{ top.question }}</i> first ({{ top.shap | f1 }}% and {{ top.lime | f1 }}% of total importance); they agreed globally (Spearman {{ agreement['Global Spearman'] | f3 }}) but less per person ({{ agreement['Per-person Spearman'] | f3 }}), and SHAP was more faithful. Conformal prediction at a 95% target covered {{ c95.actual | f2 }}% of test people. However, {{ n.twin_share | f1 }}% of test people had an identical answer pattern in the training set; with grouped cross-validation, accuracy fell to {{ n.grouped_low | f1 }}–{{ n.grouped_high | f1 }}% for the six non-linear models.</p>
  <p><strong>Conclusion.</strong> Under the random-split protocol used in earlier work, the proposed ensemble matches the published range, with explanations, uncertainty estimates and statistical tests that earlier studies lack. The duplicate audit shows that the 98–100% figures on this dataset mostly measure recognition of answer patterns already seen, and that about {{ robust[H].grouped | round | int }}% is the realistic estimate for a new pattern. Future studies on this dataset should report duplicate-aware validation next to the standard one.</p>
  <p class="keywords"><b>Keywords</b> &nbsp; postpartum anxiety · perinatal mental health · stacking ensemble · explainable AI · SHAP · LIME · conformal prediction · data leakage · duplicate-aware validation · questionnaire data</p>

</section>

