{# ================================================================== CHAPTER 1 #}
{{ open_chapter('1', 'Why another model for a dataset that already reports 99% accuracy, and what this project sets out to show instead.') }}

{{ h2('1.1') }}
<p>The weeks and months after childbirth are a period of high risk for a mother's mental health. Postpartum depression is the best-known condition, but anxiety in the same period is also common, frequently occurs together with depression, and is easier to miss because sleeplessness, worry and irritability are often dismissed as a normal part of new parenthood. Untreated, perinatal anxiety and depression affect the mother, her relationship with her partner and the early bond with the baby.</p>
<p>Screening is the first line of defence. In practice it is done with short questionnaires: a mother answers a handful of questions, and the answers decide whether a clinician looks more closely. A questionnaire is cheap, quick and can be given anywhere, which is exactly why researchers have asked whether a machine-learning model could read the answers and flag women at risk automatically. A growing body of work, reviewed in Chapter 3, has trained models on hospital records, cohort studies and questionnaire data {{ cite('6–13') }}.</p>
<p>This project works with one such questionnaire dataset: {{ '{:,}'.format(n.rows) }} responses published on Kaggle under the name <i>PostPartum Depression</i> {{ cite('30') }}. It contains an age band and nine items about mood, sleep, appetite, guilt, bonding with the baby and suicide attempt. One item, <i>Feeling anxious</i>, is the target that the dataset's own description names, and it is the answer this study predicts.</p>

{{ h2('1.2') }}
<p>Several papers have used the same {{ '{:,}'.format(n.rows) }} rows and report accuracies from {{ n.lit_low | f2 }}% to {{ n.lit_high | f1 }}% {{ cite('1–3, 5') }}. On the surface the problem looks solved. Four things are missing, however, before such a number can support a screening tool.</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>An explanation.</strong> A clinician cannot act on "the model says anxious" without knowing which answers produced that result. Most earlier studies on this dataset give no per-person explanation, and the one that shows several explanation methods side by side does not measure them against each other {{ cite('4') }}.</li>
  <li><strong>An honest statement of uncertainty.</strong> Every prediction in those papers is delivered with full confidence. A screening tool should be able to say "I am not sure" and hand the case to a human.</li>
  <li><strong>Evidence that the winning model really wins.</strong> A single accuracy on a single test split of about 300 people cannot separate 98.7% from 99.6%. Without a confidence interval and a statistical test, a ranking of models is an anecdote.</li>
  <li><strong>A check on the data itself.</strong> Of the {{ '{:,}'.format(n.rows) }} responses in this dataset, {{ '{:,}'.format(n.repeated_rows) }} repeat an answer pattern that already occurs earlier in the file. A random split therefore puts copies of the same pattern in both the training and the test set, and a model can score highly by recognising what it has already seen.</li>
</ul>
<div class="box">
  <div class="h">The central idea of this book</div>
  <p>A high accuracy is where the evaluation of a screening model starts. This project builds a competitive model and then asks four further questions of it: can it explain itself, does it know when it is unsure, is it really better than its rivals, and does its accuracy survive when it is tested on answer patterns it has never seen?</p>
</div>

{{ h2('1.3') }}
<p>The study is organised around five research questions.</p>
<div class="tbl">
  {{ cap('1.1', 'Research questions and the chapters that answer them.') }}
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th style="width:9mm"></th><th>Research question</th><th style="width:30mm">Answered in</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>RQ1</td><td>Which of seven models, from a linear baseline to a stacked hybrid, predicts the <i>Feeling anxious</i> answer best, and are the differences statistically significant?</td><td>Chapter 6</td></tr>
      <tr><td>RQ2</td><td>Which questionnaire answers drive the hybrid model's predictions, and do SHAP and LIME give the same explanation?</td><td>Chapter 7</td></tr>
      <tr><td>RQ3</td><td>When SHAP and LIME disagree, which is more faithful to the model, more stable and cheaper to compute?</td><td>Chapter 7</td></tr>
      <tr><td>RQ4</td><td>Can the model give calibrated probabilities, valid uncertainty sets, actionable counterfactuals and comparable accuracy across age groups?</td><td>Chapter 8</td></tr>
      <tr><td>RQ5</td><td>How much of the measured accuracy remains when no answer pattern is shared between training and test data?</td><td>Chapter 9</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
</div>

{{ h2('1.4') }}
<ol>
  <li>Build seven classifiers under one identical preprocessing and validation protocol so that their results are directly comparable.</li>
  <li>Propose and evaluate a heterogeneous stacking ensemble (the Hybrid Stack) that combines two boosting models, a bagging model and a neural network.</li>
  <li>Support every comparison with a confidence interval and an appropriate statistical test.</li>
  <li>Explain the hybrid's predictions with two independent methods and measure those methods against each other.</li>
  <li>Add uncertainty quantification, a counterfactual explanation and a subgroup check.</li>
  <li>Audit the dataset for repeated answer patterns and report a duplicate-aware accuracy next to the standard one.</li>
  <li>Keep the whole study reproducible: fixed seeds, pinned library versions, one notebook of {{ n.task_count }} small tasks.</li>
</ol>

{{ h2('1.5') }}
<p>The project makes five contributions.</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>A competitive, fully documented stacking ensemble.</strong> The Hybrid Stack reaches {{ test[H].accuracy | f2 }}% test accuracy with a ROC-AUC of {{ test[H].roc_auc | f3 }} and one of the best-calibrated probability outputs among the seven models.</li>
  <li><strong>A statistically honest model comparison.</strong> The tests show that six of the seven models are indistinguishable on this data, a finding that single-number comparisons in earlier work could not reveal.</li>
  <li><strong>A quantitative SHAP-versus-LIME comparison.</strong> The two methods are measured on agreement, faithfulness, stability and speed, instead of only being displayed.</li>
  <li><strong>Trustworthy-AI additions that are new for this dataset.</strong> Conformal prediction sets, a counterfactual example and an age-group accuracy check.</li>
  <li><strong>A duplicate audit.</strong> To our knowledge from the abstracts reviewed, no earlier study on this dataset analyses its repeated answer patterns. This book shows that they explain most of the gap between about 85% and 98–100%.</li>
</ul>

{{ h2('1.6') }}
<p>Chapter 2 introduces the concepts a reader needs. Chapter 3 reviews earlier work and states seven research gaps. Chapter 4 describes the dataset. Chapter 5 gives the full methodology with diagrams. Chapters 6 to 10 present the results: the model competition, the explanations, the trustworthy-AI checks, the duplicate audit and the comparison with published work. Chapter 11 discusses what the results mean and what was solved, Chapter 12 lists the limitations, Chapter 13 sets out future work and Chapter 14 concludes. Appendices give the reproduction steps, a map of the notebook, complete result tables and a glossary.</p>
</section>

{# ================================================================== CHAPTER 2 #}
{{ open_chapter('2', 'The ideas the study relies on, explained without assuming prior knowledge of any of them.') }}

{{ h2('2.1') }}
<p>A <strong>diagnosis</strong> is made by a clinician after an interview and, where needed, further assessment. A <strong>screening</strong> result only sorts people into "look closer" and "probably fine". This difference shapes how a screening model should be judged. Missing a woman who needs help (a false negative) is usually worse than asking a healthy woman a few more questions (a false positive), so recall matters as much as accuracy. It also means the label in a screening dataset is not a diagnosis. In this project the label is one self-reported questionnaire item, which limits what any model trained on it can claim.</p>

{{ h2('2.2') }}
<p>The seven models in this study belong to four families.</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Linear models.</strong> Logistic regression adds up a weight for each answer and converts the sum into a probability. It is transparent and fast, but it cannot represent interactions, such as an answer that matters only in combination with another.</li>
  <li><strong>Bagging.</strong> A random forest trains hundreds of decision trees on random resamples of the data and averages their votes. Averaging many independent trees gives a stable model that rarely overfits badly.</li>
  <li><strong>Boosting.</strong> XGBoost {{ cite('19') }}, LightGBM {{ cite('20') }} and CatBoost {{ cite('21') }} also build many trees, but one after another: each new tree concentrates on the mistakes of the trees before it. The three differ in how they grow trees and handle categories. CatBoost was designed with categorical data in mind, which suits questionnaire answers.</li>
  <li><strong>Neural networks.</strong> A multilayer perceptron (MLP) passes the inputs through layers of weighted sums and non-linear functions. With two hidden layers it is a small representative of deep learning.</li>
</ul>

{{ h2('2.3') }}
<p>Different model families make different mistakes. <strong>Stacking</strong>, introduced by Wolpert {{ cite('18') }}, exploits this. A set of <i>base models</i> each produce a probability, and a second model, the <i>meta-learner</i>, learns how to combine them. The important detail is how the meta-learner is trained. If it saw base-model predictions for the same people those models were trained on, it would learn to trust models that have merely memorised. Stacking therefore uses <strong>out-of-fold predictions</strong>: the training set is cut into folds, and each base model predicts only the fold it was not trained on. The meta-learner sees honest predictions and learns realistic weights.</p>
<p>With base models <i>f</i><sub>1</sub>, …, <i>f</i><sub>4</sub> and a logistic-regression meta-learner, the final probability for a person with answers <i>x</i> is</p>
<div class="eq">P(anxious | x) = &sigma;( w<sub>0</sub> + w<sub>1</sub> f<sub>1</sub>(x) + w<sub>2</sub> f<sub>2</sub>(x) + w<sub>3</sub> f<sub>3</sub>(x) + w<sub>4</sub> f<sub>4</sub>(x) ),</div>
<p>where &sigma;(<i>z</i>) = 1 / (1 + <i>e</i><sup>&minus;<i>z</i></sup>) is the logistic function and the weights <i>w</i> are learned from the out-of-fold probabilities.</p>

{{ h2('2.4') }}
<p>An ensemble of hundreds of trees and a neural network cannot be read like a formula. Explainable AI (XAI) methods give a post-hoc account of a single prediction: how much did each answer push the probability up or down?</p>
<h3>SHAP</h3>
<p>SHAP {{ cite('14') }} is based on Shapley values from cooperative game theory. It treats the answers as players in a game whose payout is the model's prediction and asks what each player contributed on average over all possible orders in which the players could join. The contribution of answer <i>j</i> is</p>
<div class="eq">&phi;<sub>j</sub> = &Sigma;<sub>S &sube; F \ {j}</sub> &nbsp; [ |S|! (|F| &minus; |S| &minus; 1)! / |F|! ] &nbsp; ( v(S &cup; {j}) &minus; v(S) ),</div>
<p>where <i>F</i> is the set of all features and <i>v</i>(<i>S</i>) is the model's expected output when only the answers in <i>S</i> are known. SHAP values have a property that makes them easy to check: the baseline prediction plus all contributions equals the model's output for that person exactly. With nine features the exact computation is feasible, so this study uses exact SHAP and no approximation.</p>
<h3>LIME</h3>
<p>LIME {{ cite('15') }} takes a different route. Around the person to be explained it generates many slightly changed versions of the answers, asks the model for a prediction on each, and fits a simple weighted linear model to those predictions. The weights of that local linear model are the explanation. LIME is fast and works with any model, but its output depends on random sampling, and its weights do not add up to the prediction.</p>
<h3>Why compare them</h3>
<p>The two methods can disagree, and when they do a user has no way of knowing which to trust. This is known as the disagreement problem {{ cite('16') }}. Chapter 7 therefore does not only show both explanations; it measures how often they agree, which one finds the answers that really move the model, and which one is more stable.</p>

{{ h2('2.5') }}
<p>A probability is <strong>calibrated</strong> if, among all people given a predicted probability of 0.8, about 80% really are positive. The <strong>Brier score</strong>, the mean squared difference between predicted probability and outcome, summarises calibration and accuracy together; lower is better.</p>
<p><strong>Conformal prediction</strong> {{ cite('23, 24') }} goes one step further. Instead of a single answer it returns a <i>set</i> of answers with a guarantee: if the user asks for 95% coverage, the true answer lies in the set at least about 95% of the time, whatever model is underneath. For a two-class problem the set can be one answer (the model commits), both answers (the model is unsure), or empty (no answer is plausible enough at this strictness). The method needs a nonconformity score for each calibration person, here one minus the probability the model gave to that person's true class, and a threshold taken from a quantile of those scores.</p>

{{ h2('2.6') }}
<p>SHAP and LIME describe why the model said what it said. A <strong>counterfactual explanation</strong> {{ cite('25') }} answers a more practical question: what is the smallest change to the answers that would have changed the prediction? For a woman flagged as anxious, a counterfactual such as "if irritability were <i>sometimes</i> instead of <i>yes</i>, the model would no longer flag her" tells a clinician which symptom the model's decision hinges on.</p>

{{ h2('2.7') }}
<p><strong>Data leakage</strong> is any route by which information from the test data reaches the model during training. It makes a model look better than it is and is a leading cause of irreproducible results in machine-learning-based science {{ cite('27') }}. Common routes are fitting a scaler or encoder on all data before splitting, tuning hyper-parameters on the test set, and <strong>repeated records</strong>: if the same row appears on both sides of a split, the test no longer measures prediction on new cases.</p>
<p>The remedy for repeated records is <strong>grouped validation</strong>. Rows that share the same content are given the same group label, and the split is made so that a whole group falls on one side. The test set then contains only patterns the model has never seen, which is the situation a deployed screening tool faces when a woman gives a new combination of answers.</p>
</section>

