{{ h2('4.3') }}
<p>Only {{ n.missing_cells }} of the cells are empty: {% for k, v in n.missing.items() %}{{ v }} in <i>{{ k }}</i>{% if not loop.last %}{{ ', ' if loop.revindex > 2 else ' and ' }}{% endif %}{% endfor %}. They are not imputed. A skipped question can itself carry information, so every empty cell becomes its own answer category, "No answer". No row is dropped.</p>

{{ h2('4.4') }}
<p>The target is the answer to <i>Feeling anxious</i>. Of the {{ '{:,}'.format(n.rows) }} women, {{ n.anxious }} ({{ n.anxious_share | f1 }}%) answered <i>Yes</i> and {{ n.not_anxious }} ({{ (100 - n.anxious_share) | f1 }}%) answered <i>No</i> (Figure 4.2). The imbalance is moderate, about two to one. A model that always answered "anxious" would be right {{ n.anxious_share | f1 }}% of the time, which is the floor any useful model must clear. Because of the imbalance, the study reports balanced accuracy, recall, specificity, F1 and the Matthews correlation coefficient next to plain accuracy.</p>
{{ figure('4.2', 'fig01_class_balance.png', 'Class balance of the target.', 'narrow') }}

{{ h2('4.5') }}
<p>Cramér's V measures how strongly two categorical variables are associated, from 0 (none) to 1 (perfect). Figure 4.3 shows it for each question against the target. <i>Feeling of guilt</i> stands well above the rest. No single question comes close to determining the target, so a model has to combine answers to do well, which is why the linear baseline falls far behind the tree-based models in Chapter 6.</p>
{{ figure('4.3', 'fig03_cramers_v.png', 'Cramér\'s V between each question and the target.') }}

{{ h2('4.6') }}
<p>This is the most important property of the dataset. Ignoring the timestamp, the {{ '{:,}'.format(n.rows) }} rows contain only <strong>{{ n.unique_patterns }} distinct answer patterns</strong>. The remaining {{ '{:,}'.format(n.repeated_rows) }} rows ({{ (n.repeated_rows / n.rows * 100) | f1 }}%) are exact repeats of a pattern that occurs earlier in the file. The most frequent pattern appears {{ n.largest_pattern }} times, and only {{ n.patterns_seen_once }} pattern{{ '' if n.patterns_seen_once == 1 else 's' }} appear{{ 's' if n.patterns_seen_once == 1 else '' }} exactly once, so almost every pattern in the file has at least one copy.</p>
<p>A second fact makes the repeats decisive: <strong>{% if n.mixed_label_patterns == 0 %}no feature pattern occurs with both target values{% else %}only {{ n.mixed_label_patterns }} feature patterns occur with both target values{% endif %}</strong>. Whenever two women gave the same nine answers, they also gave the same answer to <i>Feeling anxious</i>. A model that simply memorised every training pattern together with its label would therefore be right on every test row whose pattern it had already seen. Chapter 9 measures how many test rows that is and what happens to accuracy when it is prevented.</p>
<div class="box">
  <div class="h">Why the rows are kept</div>
  <p>The study does not delete the repeated rows. Earlier papers used all {{ '{:,}'.format(n.rows) }} rows with a random split, so the same protocol is needed for a fair comparison. The repeats are handled by reporting a second, duplicate-aware validation next to the standard one.</p>
</div>

{{ h2('4.7') }}
<p>The data contain self-reported mental-health answers, including a question about suicide attempt. They are public and carry no direct identifiers, but they remain sensitive. Three rules follow for this project: the raw file is never modified; results are reported only in aggregate, with individual explanations shown for anonymous test rows; and nothing in this book is a diagnostic tool or may be used to assess an individual.</p>
</section>

