{{ h2('5.2') }}
<ul>
  <li><strong>Cleaning.</strong> The timestamp is dropped because it carries no clinical meaning and could leak the order of data entry. Empty cells become the category "No answer".</li>
  <li><strong>Target.</strong> <i>Feeling anxious</i> is coded 1 for <i>Yes</i> and 0 for <i>No</i>.</li>
  <li><strong>Encoding.</strong> Each answer is first given an integer code, which keeps the data compact and lets the explanation methods work on the original nine questions. Inside every model pipeline the codes are then <strong>one-hot encoded</strong>, so no model is told that "Sometimes" lies numerically between "No" and "Yes". Because the encoder is part of the pipeline, it is refitted inside each cross-validation fold and never sees validation data.</li>
  <li><strong>Split.</strong> A stratified 80/20 split with seed 42 gives {{ '{:,}'.format(n.train_n) }} training and {{ n.test_n }} test people, both with {{ n.anxious_share | f1 }}% anxious answers. The test set is used once, at the end.</li>
  <li><strong>No scaling, no resampling.</strong> All inputs are one-hot indicators, so scaling is unnecessary. The two-to-one class ratio is handled by reporting imbalance-aware metrics instead of by synthetic oversampling, which would create further near-duplicate rows.</li>
</ul>

{{ h2('5.3') }}
<p>Seven models were chosen to cover the main families in the literature on this dataset, from a transparent baseline to the proposed hybrid. Table 5.1 lists their settings.</p>
<div class="tbl long">
