</figure>
<p>Training proceeds in three steps. First, the training set is cut into five folds, and each base model is trained five times, each time predicting the fold that was held back. This yields one honest probability per person per base model. Second, the logistic regression is fitted on those four columns of probabilities. Third, each base model is refitted on the whole training set for use at prediction time.</p>

{{ h2('5.5') }}
<p>XGBoost and LightGBM are sensitive to their settings and were tuned with Optuna {{ cite('22') }}, a Bayesian optimiser that proposes each new setting from the results of the earlier ones (a tree-structured Parzen estimator, seeded for reproducibility). Each model received 25 trials, and each trial was scored by three-fold cross-validation on the training set only. Table 5.2 gives the search ranges. The other models use fixed, conventional settings, which keeps the search modest and the risk of overfitting the validation folds low.</p>
