<ul>
  <li><strong>Tuning</strong> uses three-fold cross-validation on the training set.</li>
  <li><strong>Comparison</strong> uses ten-fold stratified cross-validation on the training set. Ten accuracies per model give a mean, a standard deviation and paired samples for the statistical tests.</li>
  <li><strong>Final measurement</strong> refits each model on the full training set and evaluates it once on the {{ n.test_n }} test people.</li>
</ul>

{{ h2('5.7') }}
<p>With TP, TN, FP and FN the counts of true positives, true negatives, false positives and false negatives, the study reports the following.</p>
<div class="tbl long">
  {{ cap('5.3', 'Performance metrics used in the study.') }}
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th style="width:36mm">Metric</th><th style="width:58mm">Definition</th><th>What it tells the reader</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Accuracy</td><td>(TP + TN) / all</td><td>Share of all people classified correctly</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Balanced accuracy</td><td>(recall + specificity) / 2</td><td>Accuracy that is not inflated by the larger class</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Precision</td><td>TP / (TP + FP)</td><td>Of those flagged anxious, how many are</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Recall (sensitivity)</td><td>TP / (TP + FN)</td><td>Of the anxious women, how many are found; the key number for screening</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Specificity</td><td>TN / (TN + FP)</td><td>Of the not-anxious women, how many are correctly cleared</td></tr>
      <tr><td>F1</td><td>Harmonic mean of precision and recall</td><td>One number balancing the two</td></tr>
      <tr><td>MCC</td><td>Correlation between prediction and truth, from &minus;1 to 1</td><td>Robust summary under class imbalance</td></tr>
      <tr><td>ROC-AUC</td><td>Area under the ROC curve</td><td>How well probabilities rank anxious above not-anxious, at every threshold</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Brier score</td><td>Mean of (probability &minus; outcome)<sup>2</sup></td><td>Quality of the probabilities themselves; lower is better</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
</div>

{{ h2('5.8') }}
<p>Four procedures test whether observed differences are real. They follow the recommendations of Demšar for comparing several classifiers {{ cite('26') }}.</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Friedman test</strong> on the ten fold accuracies of all seven models: is there any difference among the models at all?</li>
  <li><strong>Wilcoxon signed-rank tests</strong>, Hybrid Stack against each other model on the same ten folds, with <strong>Holm correction</strong> for the six comparisons.</li>
  <li><strong>McNemar test</strong> (exact binomial form) on the test set: of the people on whom two models disagree, does one model win more often than chance would allow?</li>
  <li><strong>Bootstrap confidence interval</strong>: the {{ n.test_n }} test outcomes are resampled 2,000 times to give a 95% interval for the hybrid's test accuracy.</li>
</ul>

{{ h2('5.9') }}
<p>The Hybrid Stack is explained for the first 100 test people. SHAP is computed exactly against a background of 30 randomly chosen training people. LIME uses 1,000 perturbed samples per person and treats all features as categorical. The two are then compared on four criteria.</p>
<div class="tbl long">
  {{ cap('5.4', 'The four criteria on which SHAP and LIME are compared.') }}
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th style="width:26mm">Criterion</th><th>How it is measured</th><th style="width:46mm">Better when</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Agreement</td><td>Spearman rank correlation of global importance; mean per-person rank correlation; share of people with the same top question; overlap of the top three; share of contributions with the same sign</td><td>Higher: the two tell the same story</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Faithfulness</td><td>Deletion test: replace a person's top 1, 2 or 3 questions (as ranked by the method) with the answers of the 30 background people and record the mean absolute change in predicted probability. Random choice of questions is the reference</td><td>Higher: the method found the answers that really move the model</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Stability</td><td>Repeat the explanation for 20 people with different background people (SHAP) or a different random seed (LIME); Spearman correlation between the two runs</td><td>Higher: the same person gets the same explanation twice</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Cost</td><td>Seconds per explained person</td><td>Lower</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
</div>
<p>As a cross-check that does not depend on either method, <strong>permutation importance</strong> is computed for all seven models: one question at a time is shuffled in the test set and the loss of accuracy is recorded.</p>

{{ h2('5.10') }}
<ul>
  <li><strong>Calibration.</strong> A reliability curve with eight quantile bins and the Brier score.</li>
  <li><strong>Conformal prediction.</strong> Out-of-fold probabilities of the hybrid on the training set give the calibration scores, <i>s</i> = 1 &minus; <i>p</i>(true class). For a target error rate &alpha;, the threshold <i>q</i> is the &lceil;(<i>n</i> + 1)(1 &minus; &alpha;)&rceil; / <i>n</i> quantile of those scores, and a class enters a test person's prediction set if 1 &minus; <i>p</i>(class) &le; <i>q</i>. Targets of 90%, 95% and 99% are evaluated.</li>
  <li><strong>Counterfactual.</strong> For one test person flagged anxious, an exhaustive search over all single-answer changes (and pairs, if no single change suffices) finds every change that brings the probability below 0.5. Age is excluded because it cannot be changed.</li>
  <li><strong>Subgroup check.</strong> Test accuracy of the hybrid in each of the five age bands.</li>
</ul>

{{ h2('5.11') }}
<p>The last part of the study addresses the repeated answer patterns of Section 4.6 in two ways. First, the test set is divided into people whose exact pattern also occurs in the training set ("twins") and people whose pattern does not, and accuracy is computed separately. Second, all seven models are re-evaluated with <strong>grouped five-fold cross-validation</strong> on the full dataset: every answer pattern is a group, and each group falls entirely into one fold. Figure 5.4 illustrates the difference.</p>
<figure>
  <svg viewBox="0 0 760 214" role="img">
    {% set colours = ['#2a78d6', '#eb6834', '#2e9e6b', '#8a5cc7', '#d4a017'] %}
