{# ================================================================== CHAPTER 6 #}
{{ open_chapter('6', 'Seven models, one protocol: who wins, by how much, and whether the margin means anything.') }}

{{ h2('6.1') }}
<p>Figure 6.1 shows the best three-fold accuracy found by Optuna after each trial. Both searches improve quickly and then flatten, which indicates that 25 trials were enough for this small problem. The best settings reached {{ n.xgb_best_cv | f2 }}% (XGBoost) and {{ n.lgbm_best_cv | f2 }}% (LightGBM) in three-fold cross-validation. These tuning scores are lower than the ten-fold scores reported next because each three-fold model is trained on only two thirds of the training set.</p>
{{ figure('6.1', 'fig04_optuna_progress.png', 'Best three-fold cross-validation accuracy after each Optuna trial.') }}

{{ h2('6.2') }}
<p>Table 6.1 and Figure 6.2 give the ten-fold cross-validation results on the training set. Six models lie between {{ n.cv_low | f2 }}% and {{ n.cv_high | f2 }}%, a spread of {{ (n.cv_high - n.cv_low) | f2 }} points that is smaller than the fold-to-fold standard deviation of any one of them. Logistic regression is far behind at {{ cv['Logistic Regression'].mean | f2 }}%, which confirms that the target depends on combinations of answers that a linear model cannot represent.</p>
<div class="tbl">
  {{ cap('6.1', 'Ten-fold stratified cross-validation accuracy on the training set.') }}
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Model</th><th class="r" style="width:30mm">Mean accuracy</th><th class="r" style="width:30mm">Std. deviation</th><th style="width:60mm"></th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      {% for m in cv.keys() %}
      <tr{% if m == H %} class="hl"{% endif %}><td>{{ m }}</td><td class="r">{{ cv[m].mean | f2 }}%</td><td class="r">&plusmn; {{ cv[m].std | f2 }}</td><td><span class="bar{{ ' o' if m == H else '' }}" style="width:{{ (cv[m].mean - 75) * 2.2 }}mm"></span></td></tr>
      {% endfor %}
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <div class="note">Bars start at 75% to make the differences among the top six visible.</div>
</div>
{{ figure('6.2', 'fig05_cv_accuracy.png', 'Mean ten-fold cross-validation accuracy; the error bar is the standard deviation across folds.') }}

{{ h2('6.3') }}
<p>Each model was then refitted on the full training set and evaluated once on the {{ n.test_n }} held-out people ({{ n.test_anxious }} anxious, {{ n.test_not_anxious }} not anxious). Table 6.2 gives all nine metrics.</p>
<div class="tbl">
  {{ cap('6.2', 'Test-set performance of the seven models. Accuracy, balanced accuracy, precision, recall, specificity and F1 in percent.') }}
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Model</th><th class="r">Acc.</th><th class="r">Bal. acc.</th><th class="r">Prec.</th><th class="r">Recall</th><th class="r">Spec.</th><th class="r">F1</th><th class="r">MCC</th><th class="r">AUC</th><th class="r">Brier</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      {% for m in models %}{% set t = test[m] %}
      <tr{% if m == H %} class="hl"{% endif %}><td>{{ m }}</td><td class="r">{{ t.accuracy | f2 }}</td><td class="r">{{ t.balanced_accuracy | f2 }}</td><td class="r">{{ t.precision | f2 }}</td><td class="r">{{ t.recall | f2 }}</td><td class="r">{{ t.specificity | f2 }}</td><td class="r">{{ t.f1 | f2 }}</td><td class="r">{{ t.mcc | f3 }}</td><td class="r">{{ t.roc_auc | f3 }}</td><td class="r">{{ t.brier | f3 }}</td></tr>
      {% endfor %}
    </tbody>
  </table>
</div>
{{ figure('6.3', 'fig06_test_accuracy.png', 'Test accuracy of every model. The proposed Hybrid Stack is shown in orange; the dashed line marks 97%.') }}
<p>Three points stand out.</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>{{ n.above_97 }} of the seven models exceed 97%.</strong> CatBoost classifies all {{ n.test_n }} test people correctly, the random forest misses {{ confusion['Random Forest'].fn + confusion['Random Forest'].fp }}, and the Hybrid Stack and the MLP each miss {{ n.hybrid_errors }}.</li>
  <li><strong>The Hybrid Stack reaches {{ test[H].accuracy | f2 }}%</strong> with a 95% bootstrap confidence interval of {{ n.ci }}%. The interval is more than two points wide, so test accuracies that differ by a point or less cannot be told apart on a test set of this size.</li>
  <li><strong>The hybrid's probabilities are among the best.</strong> Its Brier score of {{ test[H].brier | f3 }} is second only to CatBoost's {{ test['CatBoost'].brier | f3 }} and less than half that of the random forest ({{ test['Random Forest'].brier | f3 }}), although the forest has slightly higher accuracy. The meta-learner sharpens the probabilities of its base models.</li>
</ul>
<div class="box warn">
  <div class="h">CatBoost scores 100%. Is that a problem?</div>
  <p>On its own it is not an error in the code: the split, the pipeline and the test are clean. It is, however, not evidence of a perfect model. Chapter 9 shows that {{ n.twin_share | f1 }}% of the test people have an exact copy of their answers in the training set, and that CatBoost's accuracy on unseen patterns is {{ robust['CatBoost'].grouped | f1 }}%. The honest headline for CatBoost is its cross-validation result, {{ cv['CatBoost'].mean | f2 }}% &plusmn; {{ cv['CatBoost'].std | f2 }}, reported together with the grouped figure.</p>
</div>

{{ h2('6.4') }}
<p>Figure 6.4 compares training and test accuracy. All six non-linear models reach {{ n.nonlinear_train_min | f2 }}% on the training set, and their test accuracy is at most {{ n.max_gap | f2 }} points lower (Table 6.3). A gap this small would normally indicate little overfitting. Here it must be read with the duplicates in mind: the test set looks like the training set because much of it <i>is</i> the training set. The grouped validation in Chapter 9 is the proper overfitting check.</p>
<div class="tbl">
  {{ cap('6.3', 'Training accuracy, test accuracy and the gap between them (percentage points).') }}
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Model</th><th class="r" style="width:32mm">Train accuracy</th><th class="r" style="width:32mm">Test accuracy</th><th class="r" style="width:24mm">Gap</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      {% for m in models %}
      <tr{% if m == H %} class="hl"{% endif %}><td>{{ m }}</td><td class="r">{{ gap[m].train_accuracy | f2 }}%</td><td class="r">{{ gap[m].test_accuracy | f2 }}%</td><td class="r">{{ gap[m].gap | f2 }}</td></tr>
      {% endfor %}
    </tbody>
  </table>
</div>
{{ figure('6.4', 'fig07_train_vs_test.png', 'Training accuracy (grey) and test accuracy (blue) of every model.') }}

{{ h2('6.5') }}
<p>The ROC curves (Figure 6.5) of the six non-linear models hug the top-left corner, with areas between {{ n.auc_low | f3 }} and {{ n.auc_high | f3 }}. Logistic regression reaches {{ test['Logistic Regression'].roc_auc | f3 }}.</p>
{{ figure('6.5', 'fig08_roc_curves.png', 'ROC curves on the test set. The Hybrid Stack is drawn with a thicker line.') }}
<p>Table 6.4 gives the confusion matrix of every model, and Figure 6.6 shows the hybrid's. A notable pattern is that <strong>none of the six non-linear models produces a single false positive</strong>: every error is a missed anxious woman. The Hybrid Stack misses {{ confusion[H].fn }} of {{ n.test_anxious }} (recall {{ test[H].recall | f2 }}%). For a screening tool this is the less desirable kind of error, and Section 11.3 returns to it: the decision threshold of 0.5 could be lowered to trade a few false alarms for fewer misses.</p>
<div class="tbl">
  {{ cap('6.4', 'Confusion-matrix counts on the ' ~ n.test_n ~ ' test people.') }}
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Model</th><th class="r">True negative</th><th class="r">False positive</th><th class="r">False negative</th><th class="r">True positive</th><th class="r">Errors</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      {% for m in models %}{% set c = confusion[m] %}
      <tr{% if m == H %} class="hl"{% endif %}><td>{{ m }}</td><td class="r">{{ c.tn }}</td><td class="r">{{ c.fp }}</td><td class="r">{{ c.fn }}</td><td class="r">{{ c.tp }}</td><td class="r">{{ c.fp + c.fn }}</td></tr>
      {% endfor %}
    </tbody>
  </table>
</div>
<figure class="pair">
  <img src="{{ fig('fig09_confusion_matrix.png') }}" alt=""><img src="{{ fig('fig10_calibration.png') }}" alt="">
  <figcaption><b>Figure 6.6.</b> Left: confusion matrix of the Hybrid Stack. Right: its calibration curve; points on the dashed diagonal mean that predicted probabilities match observed rates.</figcaption>
</figure>
<p>The calibration curve lies close to the diagonal. Most predictions sit at the two ends, near 0 and near 1, which reflects how confidently the model separates the classes on this data.</p>

{{ h2('6.6') }}
<p>The Friedman test on the ten cross-validation folds rejects the hypothesis that all seven models perform equally (&chi;<sup>2</sup> = {{ n.friedman_chi2 | f2 }}, {{ n.friedman_p }}). The pairwise tests in Table 6.5 show where the difference lies.</p>
<div class="tbl">
  {{ cap('6.5', 'Hybrid Stack against each other model. Left: Wilcoxon signed-rank test on ten cross-validation folds with Holm correction. Right: exact McNemar test on the test set.') }}
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Hybrid Stack versus</th><th class="r">Mean diff. (points)</th><th class="r">Wilcoxon p</th><th class="r">Holm p</th><th class="r">Only hybrid right</th><th class="r">Only other right</th><th class="r">McNemar p</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      {% for w in wilcoxon %}{% set m = (mcnemar | selectattr('hybrid_vs', 'equalto', w.hybrid_vs) | list)[0] %}
      <tr><td>{{ w.hybrid_vs }}</td><td class="r">{{ '%+.2f' | format(w.mean_difference_points) }}</td><td class="r">{{ w.p_value | f3 }}</td><td class="r">{{ w.p_holm | f3 }}</td><td class="r">{{ m.only_hybrid_right }}</td><td class="r">{{ m.only_other_right }}</td><td class="r">{{ m.p_value | p }}</td></tr>
      {% endfor %}
    </tbody>
  </table>
</div>
<p>The Hybrid Stack is significantly better than logistic regression on both tests (Holm-corrected p = {{ wx_lr.p_holm | f3 }}; McNemar p &lt; 0.001). Against the other five models no difference is significant: every Holm-corrected p-value is {{ wx_min | f1 }}, and the smallest McNemar p-value is {{ mc_min | f3 }}. On the test set the hybrid and the MLP make exactly the same errors.</p>
<div class="box ok">
  <div class="h">Answer to RQ1</div>
  <p>CatBoost has the highest cross-validation and test accuracy, but the six non-linear models, including the proposed Hybrid Stack, cannot be separated statistically on this dataset. The Friedman test is significant only because logistic regression is far behind. The honest claim for the Hybrid Stack is therefore "as accurate as the best single models, with better-calibrated probabilities than most of them", not "more accurate".</p>
</div>
</section>

{# ================================================================== CHAPTER 7 #}
{{ open_chapter('7', 'Which answers drive the model, whether two explanation methods tell the same story, and which of them deserves more trust.') }}

{{ h2('7.1') }}
<p>Averaging the absolute SHAP values over the 100 explained test people gives the global importance in Figure 7.1. <i>{{ top.question }}</i> accounts for {{ top.shap | f1 }}% of the total, about {{ (top.shap / importance[1].shap) | round(1) }} times the share of the next question. It is followed by <i>{{ importance[1].question }}</i> ({{ importance[1].shap | f1 }}%) and <i>{{ importance[2].question }}</i> ({{ importance[2].shap | f1 }}%).</p>
{{ figure('7.1', 'fig11_shap_importance.png', 'SHAP global importance: mean absolute contribution of each question to the predicted probability of being anxious.') }}
<p>Figure 7.2 looks inside the top question: the mean SHAP value for each answer. An answer above zero pushes the prediction towards "anxious". The direction mirrors the raw data of Section 4.2, including its counter-intuitive ordering, which shows that the model has learned the dataset faithfully and that an explanation can only be as meaningful as the data behind it.</p>
{{ figure('7.2', 'fig12_shap_top_question.png', 'Mean SHAP value of each answer to the most important question.', 'narrow') }}
{{ figure('7.3', 'fig13_shap_one_person.png', 'SHAP explanation of a single test person. Orange bars push towards "anxious", blue bars away from it.') }}
<p>Figure 7.3 explains one person. The model gives her a probability of {{ n.person_probability | f3 }} of being anxious (her true answer is {{ 'Yes' if n.person_label == 1 else 'No' }}). The SHAP values add up exactly: the baseline of {{ n.shap_base | f3 }} plus contributions of {{ n.shap_sum | f3 }} gives {{ (n.shap_base + n.shap_sum) | f3 }}. This additivity is what allows a clinician to read the bars as a complete account of the prediction.</p>

{{ h2('7.2') }}
<p>LIME's global ranking (Figure 7.4) also puts <i>{{ top.question }}</i> first, with {{ top.lime | f1 }}% of the total. Figure 7.5 explains the same person as before. The largest bars match SHAP's; the smaller ones differ in size and occasionally in order.</p>
{{ figure('7.4', 'fig14_lime_importance.png', 'LIME global importance: mean absolute weight of each question.') }}
{{ figure('7.5', 'fig15_lime_one_person.png', 'LIME explanation of the same person as in Figure 7.3.') }}

{{ h2('7.3') }}
<p>Table 7.1 and Figure 7.6 put the two global rankings next to each other, and Table 7.2 measures their agreement.</p>
<div class="tbl">
  {{ cap('7.1', 'Share of total importance assigned to each question by SHAP and by LIME (percent).') }}
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Question</th><th class="r" style="width:18mm">SHAP</th><th style="width:44mm"></th><th class="r" style="width:18mm">LIME</th><th style="width:44mm"></th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      {% for r in importance %}
      <tr><td>{{ r.question }}</td><td class="r">{{ r.shap | f1 }}</td><td><span class="bar" style="width:{{ r.shap }}mm"></span></td><td class="r">{{ r.lime | f1 }}</td><td><span class="bar o" style="width:{{ r.lime }}mm"></span></td></tr>
      {% endfor %}
    </tbody>
  </table>
</div>
{{ figure('7.6', 'fig16_shap_vs_lime.png', 'SHAP and LIME global importance side by side.') }}
<div class="tbl">
  {{ cap('7.2', 'Agreement between SHAP and LIME over 100 test people.') }}
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Measure</th><th class="r" style="width:22mm">Value</th><th>Reading</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Spearman correlation of the global rankings</td><td class="r">{{ agreement['Global Spearman'] | f3 }}</td><td>Very strong: the same overall picture</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Mean per-person Spearman correlation</td><td class="r">{{ agreement['Per-person Spearman'] | f3 }}</td><td>Moderate: individual explanations differ</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Same top question</td><td class="r">{{ agreement['Same top question (%)'] | f1 }}%</td><td>For about {{ (10 - agreement['Same top question (%)'] / 10) | round | int }} people in 10 the two methods name a different main reason</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Overlap of the top three questions</td><td class="r">{{ agreement['Top-3 overlap (%)'] | f1 }}%</td><td>On average about two of the three match</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Contributions with the same sign</td><td class="r">{{ agreement['Same direction (%)'] | f1 }}%</td><td>About one contribution in {{ (100 / (100 - agreement['Same direction (%)'])) | round | int }} points the opposite way</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
</div>
<p>The two methods agree on the big picture and differ in the detail. A reader who only saw the global bar charts would conclude that SHAP and LIME are interchangeable. At the level where a clinician would use them, one woman at a time, they name a different main reason for roughly {{ (100 - agreement['Same top question (%)']) | round | int }}% of people. This is the disagreement problem {{ cite('16') }} made concrete, and it is why the next section asks which method is right more often.</p>

{{ h2('7.4') }}
<p>Faithfulness is tested by deletion. If an explanation has found the answers that really drive a prediction, replacing those answers should change the prediction a lot. Table 7.3 gives the mean absolute change in predicted probability after replacing the top one, two and three questions named by each method, with random questions as the reference; Figure 7.7 shows the same numbers.</p>
<div class="tbl">
  {{ cap('7.3', 'Deletion-based faithfulness: mean absolute change in predicted probability after replacing the top-ranked questions. Higher is better.') }}
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Questions replaced</th><th class="r">SHAP</th><th class="r">LIME</th><th class="r">Random</th><th class="r">SHAP over LIME</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      {% for r in faith %}
      <tr><td>Top {{ r.k }}</td><td class="r">{{ r.SHAP | f3 }}</td><td class="r">{{ r.LIME | f3 }}</td><td class="r">{{ r.Random | f3 }}</td><td class="r">+{{ ((r.SHAP / r.LIME - 1) * 100) | round | int }}%</td></tr>
      {% endfor %}
    </tbody>
  </table>
</div>
{{ figure('7.7', 'fig17_faithfulness.png', 'Faithfulness of SHAP, LIME and a random ranking.', 'narrow') }}
<p>Both methods are far better than chance: replacing a single question picked by SHAP moves the prediction by {{ faith[0].SHAP | f3 }}, about {{ (faith[0].SHAP / faith[0].Random) | round | int }} times the effect of a random question. SHAP is ahead of LIME at every depth, by {{ ((faith[0].SHAP / faith[0].LIME - 1) * 100) | round | int }}% for the top question and {{ ((faith[2].SHAP / faith[2].LIME - 1) * 100) | round | int }}% for the top three. When the two disagree about a person, SHAP's ranking is the one that better reflects what the model actually does.</p>

{{ h2('7.5') }}
<div class="tbl">
  {{ cap('7.4', 'SHAP versus LIME: summary of all criteria.') }}
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Criterion</th><th style="width:52mm">SHAP</th><th style="width:52mm">LIME</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Most important question</td><td>{{ xai['Top question'].SHAP }} ({{ top.shap | f1 }}%)</td><td>{{ xai['Top question'].LIME }} ({{ top.lime | f1 }}%)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Top three questions</td><td>{{ xai['Top-3 questions'].SHAP }}</td><td>{{ xai['Top-3 questions'].LIME }}</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Faithfulness, top 1 replaced</td><td>{{ xai['Faithfulness (top-1 removed)'].SHAP | f3 }}</td><td>{{ xai['Faithfulness (top-1 removed)'].LIME | f3 }}</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Faithfulness, top 3 replaced</td><td>{{ xai['Faithfulness (top-3 removed)'].SHAP | f3 }}</td><td>{{ xai['Faithfulness (top-3 removed)'].LIME | f3 }}</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Stability (Spearman between two runs)</td><td>{{ xai['Stability (Spearman)'].SHAP | f3 }}</td><td>{{ xai['Stability (Spearman)'].LIME | f3 }}</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Seconds per person</td><td>{{ xai['Seconds per person'].SHAP | f2 }}</td><td>{{ xai['Seconds per person'].LIME | f2 }}</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Contributions add up to the prediction</td><td>{{ xai['Adds up to the prediction'].SHAP }}</td><td>{{ xai['Adds up to the prediction'].LIME }}</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <div class="note">Timings depend on the machine and vary from run to run; their ratio is the meaningful quantity.</div>
</div>
<p>The two methods are about equally stable: repeating the explanation with different background people (SHAP) or a different random seed (LIME) gives rank correlations of {{ xai['Stability (Spearman)'].SHAP | f3 }} and {{ xai['Stability (Spearman)'].LIME | f3 }}. LIME is roughly {{ speed | round | int }} times faster. With nine features both are quick enough for interactive use, so speed is not a reason to prefer LIME here.</p>
<div class="box ok">
  <div class="h">Answer to RQ2 and RQ3</div>
  <p><i>{{ top.question }}</i> is the dominant driver of the model's predictions according to both methods. SHAP ranks <i>{{ importance[1].question }}</i>, <i>{{ importance[2].question }}</i> and <i>{{ importance[3].question }}</i> next. SHAP and LIME agree on this global picture but give a different main reason for about {{ (100 - agreement['Same top question (%)']) | round | int }}% of individuals. SHAP is the more faithful of the two, equally stable, slower but still fast, and the only one whose contributions sum to the prediction. For per-person explanations of this model, SHAP is the method to use.</p>
</div>

{{ h2('7.6') }}
<p>Permutation importance offers a check that is independent of both SHAP and LIME and can be computed for every model. Table 7.5 gives the accuracy lost, in percentage points, when one question is shuffled in the test set.</p>
<div class="tbl">
  {{ cap('7.5', 'Permutation importance by model: loss of test accuracy (percentage points) when one question is shuffled. Darker cells mean larger loss.') }}
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Question</th><th class="r">LR</th><th class="r">RF</th><th class="r">XGB</th><th class="r">LGBM</th><th class="r">CatB</th><th class="r">MLP</th><th class="r">Hybrid</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      {% for r in permutation %}
      <tr><td>{{ r.question }}</td>{% for m in ['Logistic Regression', 'Random Forest', 'XGBoost', 'LightGBM', 'CatBoost', 'MLP Neural Net', H] %}{% set v = r[m] %}<td class="r" style="background: rgba(42,120,214,{{ ([v, 0] | max) / permutation_max * 0.55 }})">{{ v | f1 }}</td>{% endfor %}</tr>
      {% endfor %}
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <div class="note">LR = logistic regression, RF = random forest, XGB = XGBoost, LGBM = LightGBM, CatB = CatBoost.</div>
</div>
<p>All seven models, including the linear baseline, lose most accuracy when <i>{{ permutation[0].question }}</i> is shuffled: about {{ permutation[0][H] | round | int }} points for the hybrid. The models agree less on the order of the remaining questions, and the MLP spreads its reliance more evenly than the tree models. The agreement on the top question across seven different models and three different importance methods is strong evidence that its dominance is a property of the data and not an artefact of one model or one explanation technique.</p>
</section>

{# ================================================================== CHAPTER 8 #}
{{ open_chapter('8', 'Three additions that a screening tool needs beyond accuracy and explanation: knowing when it is unsure, saying what would change its mind, and treating subgroups alike.') }}

{{ h2('8.1') }}
<p>Table 8.1 and Figure 8.1 give the conformal prediction results for three coverage targets. In every case the coverage achieved on the test set is at or above the coverage promised.</p>
<div class="tbl">
  {{ cap('8.1', 'Conformal prediction with the Hybrid Stack on the ' ~ n.test_n ~ ' test people.') }}
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th class="r">Target coverage</th><th class="r">Actual coverage</th><th class="r">Mean set size</th><th class="r">One answer</th><th class="r">Both answers</th><th class="r">Empty set</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      {% for r in conformal %}
      <tr><td class="r">{{ r.target | round | int }}%</td><td class="r">{{ r.actual | f2 }}%</td><td class="r">{{ r.size | f2 }}</td><td class="r">{{ r.single | f2 }}%</td><td class="r">{{ r.both | f2 }}%</td><td class="r">{{ r.empty | f2 }}%</td></tr>
      {% endfor %}
    </tbody>
  </table>
</div>
{{ figure('8.1', 'fig19_conformal_coverage.png', 'Promised and achieved coverage of the conformal prediction sets.', 'narrow') }}
<p>At the 95% target the model commits to a single answer for {{ c95.single | f1 }}% of the test people and returns an empty set for the remaining {{ c95.empty | f1 }}%. {% if c95.both == 0 and (c95.actual - c95.single) | abs < 0.01 %}Every single-answer set contains the true answer, so all of the model's test errors fall among the people for whom it declined to commit.{% endif %} An empty set arises because the model is confident so often that the calibrated threshold becomes strict; a prediction with a probability near 0.5 then fails the threshold for both classes. In practice an empty set and a two-answer set mean the same thing to a clinic: this case goes to a human. At the 99% target the behaviour changes as theory predicts: the empty sets disappear and {{ conformal[2].both | f1 }}% of people receive both answers.</p>
<div class="box">
  <div class="h">What this adds</div>
  <p>Without conformal prediction the model answers all {{ n.test_n }} people and is wrong on {{ n.hybrid_errors }} of them with no warning. With it, at the 95% setting, the model answers about {{ (n.test_n * c95.single / 100) | round | int }} people and refers about {{ (n.test_n * (c95.empty + c95.both) / 100) | round | int }} to a human, and the cases it refers include the ones it would have got wrong.</p>
</div>

{{ h2('8.2') }}
<p>For one test person whom the model flags as anxious with probability {{ n.cf_probability | f3 }}, the search of Section 5.10 found that a single changed answer is enough to reverse the decision (Table 8.2).</p>
<div class="tbl">
  {{ cap('8.2', 'Counterfactuals for one test person predicted anxious with probability ' ~ (n.cf_probability | f3) ~ '.') }}
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th class="r" style="width:30mm">Answers changed</th><th>Change</th><th class="r" style="width:36mm">New probability</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      {% for r in counterfactual %}
      <tr><td class="r">{{ r.answers_changed }}</td><td>{{ r.change | replace('->', '&rarr;') }}</td><td class="r">{{ r.new_probability | f3 }}</td></tr>
      {% endfor %}
    </tbody>
  </table>
</div>
<p>The model's decision for this woman hinges on one answer. That is useful to know in two ways. For the clinician it identifies the symptom to ask about first. For the model's developer it is a warning about sensitivity: a prediction that moves from {{ n.cf_probability | f2 }} to {{ counterfactual[0].new_probability | f2 }} when one answer shifts by one step rests on a narrow basis, and a mis-clicked answer on a form could flip the outcome. A counterfactual describes the model, not the woman: it does not say that reducing irritability would relieve her anxiety.</p>

{{ h2('8.3') }}
<p>Table 8.3 and Figure 8.2 give the hybrid's test accuracy in each age band.</p>
<div class="tbl">
  {{ cap('8.3', 'Test accuracy of the Hybrid Stack by age group.') }}
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Age group</th><th class="r">Test people</th><th class="r">Accuracy</th><th class="r">Errors</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      {% for r in fairness %}
      <tr><td>{{ r.age }}</td><td class="r">{{ r.people }}</td><td class="r">{{ r.accuracy | f2 }}%</td><td class="r">{{ (r.people * (100 - r.accuracy) / 100) | round | int }}</td></tr>
      {% endfor %}
    </tbody>
  </table>
</div>
{{ figure('8.2', 'fig20_fairness_by_age.png', 'Hybrid Stack accuracy in each age group.', 'narrow') }}
<p>Accuracy ranges from {{ fairness | map(attribute='accuracy') | min | f2 }}% to {{ fairness | map(attribute='accuracy') | max | f2 }}%. The lowest value belongs to the smallest group, where it corresponds to only {{ ((fairness | sort(attribute='accuracy') | first).people * (100 - (fairness | sort(attribute='accuracy') | first).accuracy) / 100) | round | int }} misclassified people out of {{ (fairness | sort(attribute='accuracy') | first).people }}. With groups of {{ fairness | map(attribute='people') | min }} to {{ fairness | map(attribute='people') | max }} people, differences of this size are within what chance alone would produce, so the check finds no evidence of an age-related gap. It also cannot rule one out. Age is the only demographic variable in the data, so fairness with respect to income, education, ethnicity or region cannot be examined at all.</p>
<div class="box ok">
  <div class="h">Answer to RQ4</div>
  <p>Yes, within the limits of the data. The hybrid's probabilities are well calibrated (Brier {{ test[H].brier | f3 }}), its conformal sets meet their coverage promise at all three levels, a one-answer counterfactual exists for the example person, and accuracy does not differ detectably between age groups.</p>
</div>
</section>

{# ================================================================== CHAPTER 9 #}
{{ open_chapter('9', 'The check that changes how every accuracy in this book, and in earlier papers on this dataset, should be read.') }}

{{ h2('9.1') }}
<p>Section 4.6 showed that the {{ '{:,}'.format(n.rows) }} rows contain only {{ n.unique_patterns }} distinct answer patterns. After the random 80/20 split, <strong>{{ n.twins }} of the {{ n.test_n }} test people ({{ n.twin_share | f1 }}%) have an identical answer pattern in the training set</strong>. Only {{ n.no_twin_n }} test people present a pattern the model has not seen.</p>
<div class="tbl">
  {{ cap('9.1', 'Hybrid Stack test accuracy, split by whether the person\'s answer pattern also occurs in the training set.') }}
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Test people</th><th class="r">Number</th><th class="r">Share of test set</th><th class="r">Hybrid accuracy</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>With a twin in the training set</td><td class="r">{{ n.twins }}</td><td class="r">{{ n.twin_share | f1 }}%</td><td class="r">{{ n.twin_accuracy | f2 }}%</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Without a twin</td><td class="r">{{ n.no_twin_n }}</td><td class="r">{{ (100 - n.twin_share) | f1 }}%</td><td class="r">{{ n.no_twin_accuracy | f2 }}%</td></tr>
      <tr class="hl"><td>All</td><td class="r">{{ n.test_n }}</td><td class="r">100%</td><td class="r">{{ test[H].accuracy | f2 }}%</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
</div>
<p>The hybrid is right on {% if n.twin_accuracy == 100 %}every one{% else %}{{ n.twin_accuracy | f1 }}%{% endif %} of the {{ n.twins }} people with a twin and on {{ n.no_twin_right }} of the {{ n.no_twin_n }} without one. {% if n.twin_accuracy == 100 %}All {{ n.hybrid_errors }} of its test errors fall in the small group of unseen patterns.{% endif %} A group of {{ n.no_twin_n }} people is too small for a precise estimate, but the direction is unmistakable: the overall test accuracy is an average dominated by cases the model has effectively memorised.</p>

{{ h2('9.2') }}
<p>Grouped five-fold cross-validation gives a larger and more reliable sample of the same situation. Every answer pattern is confined to one fold, so each model is always tested on patterns absent from its training data. Table 9.2 and Figure 9.1 compare the result with standard cross-validation.</p>
<div class="tbl">
  {{ cap('9.2', 'Standard ten-fold cross-validation against grouped five-fold cross-validation (no answer pattern shared between training and test folds).') }}
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Model</th><th class="r">Standard CV</th><th class="r">Grouped CV</th><th class="r">Drop (points)</th><th class="r">Grouped balanced acc.</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      {% for m in robust_order %}{% set r = robust[m] %}
      <tr{% if m == H %} class="hl"{% endif %}><td>{{ m }}</td><td class="r">{{ r.standard | f2 }}%</td><td class="r">{{ r.grouped | f2 }}%</td><td class="r">{{ (r.standard - r.grouped) | f1 }}</td><td class="r">{{ r.grouped_balanced | f2 }}%</td></tr>
      {% endfor %}
    </tbody>
  </table>
</div>
{{ figure('9.1', 'fig21_standard_vs_grouped_cv.png', 'Standard cross-validation (blue) against duplicate-aware grouped cross-validation (orange).') }}
<p>Three results follow.</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>All six non-linear models lose {{ n.drop_low | round | int }} to {{ n.drop_high | round | int }} points.</strong> They fall from about 98% to between {{ n.grouped_low | f1 }}% and {{ n.grouped_high | f1 }}%. The Hybrid Stack reaches {{ robust[H].grouped | f2 }}%.</li>
  <li><strong>Logistic regression barely moves</strong> ({{ robust['Logistic Regression'].standard | f1 }}% to {{ robust['Logistic Regression'].grouped | f1 }}%). A linear model cannot memorise individual patterns, so it gains nothing from seeing them twice. The gap between it and the flexible models shrinks from about {{ (cv[H].mean - cv['Logistic Regression'].mean) | round | int }} points to about {{ (robust[H].grouped - robust['Logistic Regression'].grouped) | round | int }}.</li>
  <li><strong>The ranking changes.</strong> {{ n.grouped_best }} is best on unseen patterns ({{ n.grouped_high | f2 }}%), and the order among the top models differs from the standard ranking. No model that looks best under the random split can be assumed best in deployment.</li>
</ul>
<p>The balanced accuracies are {{ n.bal_gap_low | f1 }} to {{ n.bal_gap_high | f1 }} points below the plain accuracies, so on unseen patterns the models also become less even-handed between the two classes.</p>

{{ h2('9.3') }}
<p>The two numbers answer different questions, and both are correct answers to their own question.</p>
<div class="tbl">
  {{ cap('9.3', 'What each accuracy figure measures.') }}
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th style="width:38mm">Figure</th><th style="width:38mm">Protocol</th><th>Question it answers</th><th style="width:40mm">Use it for</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>{{ test[H].accuracy | f1 }}–{{ test['CatBoost'].accuracy | round | int }}% (test), about 98% (CV)</td><td>Random split of all rows</td><td>How often is the model right on a new row drawn from this file, most of which repeat rows it has seen?</td><td>Comparison with earlier papers, which used this protocol</td></tr>
      <tr><td>{{ n.grouped_low | f1 }}–{{ n.grouped_high | f1 }}%</td><td>Grouped split by answer pattern</td><td>How often is the model right for a woman whose combination of answers it has never seen?</td><td>Any statement about real-world screening performance</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
</div>
<div class="box warn">
  <div class="h">Reading CatBoost's 100%</div>
  <p>Because no answer pattern in this dataset occurs with both target values, a model flexible enough to memorise the training patterns is guaranteed to be right on every test person with a twin. CatBoost does this completely and also happens to be right on the {{ n.no_twin_n }} people without a twin in this particular split. Its grouped accuracy of {{ robust['CatBoost'].grouped | f2 }}% shows what remains without the twins. A score of 100% here signals a property of the data, not a perfect classifier, and reporting it without the grouped figure would mislead.</p>
</div>
<div class="box ok">
  <div class="h">Answer to RQ5</div>
  <p>When no answer pattern is shared between training and test data, the accuracy of the proposed model falls from {{ cv[H].mean | f1 }}% to {{ robust[H].grouped | f1 }}%. Roughly {{ (cv[H].mean - robust[H].grouped) | round | int }} percentage points of the headline figure come from recognising repeated patterns. The remaining {{ robust[H].grouped | round | int }}% is still well above the {{ n.anxious_share | f1 }}% majority-class floor and above the linear baseline, so the model has learned real structure, only less than the headline suggests.</p>
</div>
</section>

