{# ================================================================= CHAPTER 11 #}
{{ open_chapter('11', 'What the results mean together, which problems the project solved, and how the findings should be presented to others.') }}

{{ h2('11.1') }}
<div class="tbl">
  {{ cap('11.1', 'Summary of answers to the research questions.') }}
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th style="width:9mm"></th><th>Answer</th><th style="width:44mm">Key evidence</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>RQ1</td><td>CatBoost scores highest, but the six non-linear models are statistically indistinguishable. The Hybrid Stack matches the best single models and has better-calibrated probabilities than most.</td><td>Test {{ test[H].accuracy | f2 }}% (CI {{ n.ci }}%); Holm p = {{ wx_min | f1 }} against five models</td></tr>
      <tr><td>RQ2</td><td><i>{{ top.question }}</i> dominates. SHAP and LIME agree globally but differ for individuals.</td><td>Global Spearman {{ agreement['Global Spearman'] | f3 }}; per-person {{ agreement['Per-person Spearman'] | f3 }}</td></tr>
      <tr><td>RQ3</td><td>SHAP is more faithful and additive; stability is similar; LIME is faster.</td><td>Top-1 faithfulness {{ faith[0].SHAP | f3 }} vs {{ faith[0].LIME | f3 }}</td></tr>
      <tr><td>RQ4</td><td>Probabilities are calibrated, conformal sets meet their promise, a one-answer counterfactual exists, and no age gap is detectable.</td><td>Brier {{ test[H].brier | f3 }}; coverage {{ c95.actual | f1 }}% at a 95% target</td></tr>
      <tr><td>RQ5</td><td>About {{ (cv[H].mean - robust[H].grouped) | round | int }} points of accuracy come from repeated answer patterns.</td><td>Grouped CV {{ robust[H].grouped | f1 }}% vs standard {{ cv[H].mean | f1 }}%</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
</div>
<p>Taken together, the results describe a dataset on which any sufficiently flexible model reaches about 98% under a random split, because most test rows are copies of training rows, and about 85% on new patterns. Within that setting, the choice among modern models matters little for accuracy. What distinguishes a useful model is the quality of its probabilities, its explanations and its ability to abstain, and on those the Hybrid Stack performs well.</p>
<p>The hybrid did not beat its best base model. This is a common and honest outcome of stacking: when the base models are already near the ceiling and make almost the same errors, there is little for a meta-learner to add. What stacking did deliver is robustness. The hybrid is never the worst of the strong models on any metric, and its Brier score is better than that of three of its four base models.</p>

{{ h2('11.2') }}
<p>Table 11.2 lists the problems the project set out to solve, what was done and how far each is solved.</p>
<div class="tbl long">
  {{ cap('11.2', 'Problems addressed, the solution implemented and its status.') }}
