    </tbody>
  </table>
</div>

{{ h2('11.3') }}
<ul>
  <li><strong>For researchers using this dataset.</strong> Report grouped validation next to the random split. A new architecture that moves random-split accuracy from 98% to 99% has shown very little; one that moves grouped accuracy from 85% to 90% would have shown something real.</li>
  <li><strong>For anyone building a screening aid.</strong> The six non-linear models make no false positives and a few false negatives at the default threshold of 0.5. In screening, a missed case costs more than a false alarm, so the threshold should be chosen deliberately, on validation data, to reach a target recall.</li>
  <li><strong>For users of explanations.</strong> Two popular methods name a different main reason for a substantial minority of people. An explanation shown to a clinician should come from the more faithful method and should not be presented as a fact about the patient.</li>
  <li><strong>For interpreting feature importance.</strong> The dominant question behaves in this dataset in a way that contradicts clinical expectation. A model can be accurate and well explained and still encode a relationship that would not hold elsewhere.</li>
</ul>

{{ h2('11.4') }}
<p>When this work is written up for a journal or presented, the following order keeps the claims in proportion to the evidence.</p>
<ol>
  <li>Lead with the cross-validation result as mean and standard deviation: Hybrid Stack {{ cv[H].mean | f2 }}% &plusmn; {{ cv[H].std | f2 }}; CatBoost {{ cv['CatBoost'].mean | f2 }}% &plusmn; {{ cv['CatBoost'].std | f2 }}. Ten splits are more reliable than one.</li>
  <li>Give the single test-set result with its confidence interval, including CatBoost's 100%, and state in the same paragraph that {{ n.twin_share | f1 }}% of test people have a twin in the training set.</li>
  <li>Give the grouped cross-validation result ({{ n.grouped_low | f1 }}–{{ n.grouped_high | f1 }}%) as the estimate for unseen answer patterns.</li>
  <li>State that the proposed model is not significantly more accurate than the best single models, and present explanation quality, calibration, uncertainty and the duplicate audit as the contributions.</li>
</ol>
<div class="box">
  <div class="h">A sentence that can be used as written</div>
  <p>"Under the random-split protocol of earlier work, the proposed stacking ensemble achieved {{ test[H].accuracy | f2 }}% test accuracy (95% CI {{ n.ci }}%) and {{ cv[H].mean | f2 }}% in ten-fold cross-validation, statistically indistinguishable from CatBoost and random forest. Because {{ (n.repeated_rows / n.rows * 100) | round | int }}% of the records repeat an earlier answer pattern, we additionally report grouped cross-validation, under which accuracy was {{ robust[H].grouped | f1 }}%."</p>
</div>
</section>

{# ================================================================= CHAPTER 12 #}
{{ open_chapter('12', 'What this study cannot show, stated as plainly as what it can.') }}

{{ h2('12.1') }}
<ul>
  <li><strong>The label is not a diagnosis.</strong> The target is one self-reported questionnaire item. The model predicts how a woman answers one question from how she answers nine others. That is related to, but not the same as, detecting an anxiety disorder.</li>
  <li><strong>Provenance is undocumented.</strong> The dataset's listing gives little detail on how, where and from whom the data were collected. All timestamps fall within {{ n.collection_hours | f1 }} hours, and {{ (n.repeated_rows / n.rows * 100) | round | int }}% of rows are repeats. Whether the repeats are genuine identical answers, duplicated entries or generated records cannot be determined from the file.</li>
  <li><strong>The effective sample is small.</strong> There are {{ n.unique_patterns }} distinct answer patterns, not {{ '{:,}'.format(n.rows) }} independent observations. Grouped validation estimates performance from these.</li>
  <li><strong>A counter-intuitive main predictor.</strong> The strongest question relates to the target in the opposite direction from clinical expectation, which raises doubts about how the item was worded, coded or answered.</li>
  <li><strong>Few features and one demographic.</strong> There is no information on parity, time since birth, history of mental illness, social support, income or region, so neither richer prediction nor a serious fairness analysis is possible.</li>
  <li><strong>No external data.</strong> All results come from one file. Nothing is known about how the model would perform on women from another hospital or country.</li>
</ul>

{{ h2('12.2') }}
<ul>
  <li><strong>One train/test split.</strong> The final test uses a single split of {{ n.test_n }} people. The confidence interval reflects sampling of test people but not the variation that a different split would produce.</li>
  <li><strong>Tuning and model selection used random splits.</strong> Optuna and the ten-fold comparison ran on random folds, which contain twins. The selected hyper-parameters may therefore favour memorisation. Grouped validation was applied only at the end, as a check, with the same settings.</li>
  <li><strong>Small tuning budget.</strong> Only two models were tuned, with 25 trials each. The others use fixed settings, so the comparison is between reasonable configurations, not between each model's best possible form.</li>
  <li><strong>Explanations cover 100 people and one model.</strong> The SHAP-versus-LIME findings apply to the Hybrid Stack on this dataset. Stability was measured on 20 people.</li>
  <li><strong>Faithfulness is measured in one way.</strong> The deletion test replaces answers with those of background people, which is the same idea SHAP itself is built on and may favour it.</li>
  <li><strong>Conformal calibration uses out-of-fold scores from random folds.</strong> The coverage guarantee assumes that calibration and test data are exchangeable, which holds for the random split but would not hold for women with unseen patterns.</li>
  <li><strong>One counterfactual example.</strong> It illustrates the method and is not a systematic evaluation.</li>
  <li><strong>Literature numbers are second-hand.</strong> They come from abstracts and summaries and several details are unconfirmed.</li>
</ul>

