{{ h2('13.1') }}
<ul>
  <li><strong>Use grouped validation from the start.</strong> Tune hyper-parameters and select models with grouped folds, so that the pipeline is optimised for unseen patterns and not for memorisation. Simpler, more regularised models may then win.</li>
  <li><strong>Repeat and nest.</strong> Replace the single test split with repeated grouped cross-validation, and nest the tuning inside it, to obtain confidence intervals that include split-to-split variation.</li>
  <li><strong>Choose the threshold for screening.</strong> Select the decision threshold to reach a stated recall, for example 95%, and report the specificity that results.</li>
  <li><strong>Evaluate counterfactuals systematically.</strong> Compute them for every flagged person and report how many answers must change, which answers change most often and how plausible the changes are.</li>
  <li><strong>Broaden the XAI comparison.</strong> Add further methods such as Anchors and model-specific TreeSHAP, a second faithfulness measure that does not rely on background replacement, and explanations for the other strong models.</li>
  <li><strong>Deduplicated analysis.</strong> Train and test on the {{ n.unique_patterns }} unique patterns alone, with pattern frequency as a weight, as a third view next to the random and grouped protocols.</li>
  <li><strong>Conformal prediction under shift.</strong> Calibrate with grouped folds and examine whether coverage holds for unseen patterns.</li>
</ul>

{{ h2('13.2') }}
<ul>
  <li><strong>A dataset with documented provenance.</strong> Collect or obtain data with a clear protocol, informed consent, one record per woman and a known collection period.</li>
  <li><strong>Validated instruments.</strong> Use established scales such as the Edinburgh Postnatal Depression Scale and the GAD-7, whose scores have known clinical meaning, instead of single ad-hoc items.</li>
  <li><strong>A clinical reference standard.</strong> Take the outcome from a clinician's assessment, so that the model predicts a diagnosis and not another questionnaire answer.</li>
  <li><strong>Richer predictors.</strong> Add time since birth, parity, obstetric complications, psychiatric history, social support and socio-economic variables. These also make a real fairness analysis possible.</li>
  <li><strong>External validation.</strong> Test the frozen model on data from a different site before making any claim about generalisation.</li>
</ul>

{{ h2('13.3') }}
<ul>
  <li><strong>Prospective evaluation.</strong> Run the model alongside routine screening in a pilot and compare its referrals with clinicians' decisions, without letting it influence care.</li>
  <li><strong>Explanations designed with clinicians.</strong> Study which form of explanation (contribution bars, counterfactual sentences, uncertainty flags) clinicians understand and find useful.</li>
  <li><strong>Reporting and governance.</strong> Follow TRIPOD+AI {{ cite('28') }} for reporting and trustworthy-AI guidance such as FUTURE-AI {{ cite('29') }} for design; obtain ethical approval; plan for monitoring of performance and drift after deployment.</li>
  <li><strong>Local-language deployment.</strong> A screening aid for use in Bangladesh or similar settings needs questionnaires validated in the local language and culture, and an interface usable by community health workers.</li>
</ul>
</section>

{# ================================================================= CHAPTER 14 #}
{{ open_chapter('14', 'The study in four paragraphs.') }}
<p>This project set out to do more than add another high accuracy to a dataset that already has several. It built seven models under one protocol, proposed a heterogeneous stacking ensemble, and then examined that ensemble from every side a screening tool should be examined from.</p>
<p>Under the random-split protocol of earlier work, the Hybrid Stack reached {{ test[H].accuracy | f2 }}% test accuracy (95% CI {{ n.ci }}%) and {{ cv[H].mean | f2 }}% in ten-fold cross-validation, inside the published range of {{ n.lit_low | f2 }}% to {{ n.lit_high | f1 }}%. Statistical tests showed that it is clearly better than a linear baseline and not distinguishable from CatBoost, random forest, LightGBM, XGBoost or a neural network. Its probabilities are well calibrated, its conformal prediction sets meet their coverage promise, and both SHAP and LIME identify the same dominant question, with SHAP giving the more faithful per-person explanations.</p>
<p>The most consequential finding concerns the data. Only {{ n.unique_patterns }} of the {{ '{:,}'.format(n.rows) }} responses are distinct, {{ n.twin_share | f1 }}% of test people have an exact twin in the training set, and when no pattern is shared between training and test data, accuracy falls to {{ n.grouped_low | f1 }}–{{ n.grouped_high | f1 }}%. The 98–100% figures reported on this dataset, here and elsewhere, mostly measure recognition of answers already seen. About {{ robust[H].grouped | round | int }}% is the realistic estimate for a new answer pattern.</p>
<p>The lasting contribution is therefore methodological. Accuracy on this benchmark should be reported with a duplicate-aware validation, a confidence interval and a statistical test; explanations should be measured and not only shown; and a screening model should be able to say when it does not know. With better data, the same framework can be carried from a public benchmark towards a tool that clinicians could trust.</p>
</section>

