<section class="chapter" id="appA"><header><div class="label">Appendix A</div><h1>Reproducing the Study</h1><p class="lead">Everything needed to regenerate every number, figure and table in this book.</p></header>
<h3>Environment</h3>
<p>Python {{ n.python.rsplit('.', 1)[0] }} with the pinned versions of Table 5.5. From the project folder:</p>
<div class="box" style="font-family:var(--mono);font-size:8.6pt;line-height:1.6">
python3.11 -m venv .venv &amp;&amp; source .venv/bin/activate<br>
pip install -r requirements.txt<br>
cd notebooks &amp;&amp; jupyter lab postpartum_anxiety_xai_study.ipynb &nbsp;&nbsp;# Run All
</div>
<p>Select the project's <code>.venv</code> as the notebook kernel. Running on a different Python or library version changes a few numbers slightly (for example the XGBoost test accuracy), because tree-building and random-number details differ between versions. The run takes a few minutes and rewrites every file in <code>results/</code>.</p>
<h3>Rebuilding this book</h3>
<div class="box" style="font-family:var(--mono);font-size:8.6pt;line-height:1.6">
pip install jinja2 pandas pymupdf<br>
python book/build_book.py
</div>
<p>The build script reads the result tables, the raw data file and the printed output of the executed notebook, fills them into the book template and prints it to PDF with Google Chrome. No number in the book is typed by hand.</p>
<h3>Project layout</h3>
<div class="tbl">
  {{ cap('A.1', 'Folders of the project.') }}
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th style="width:40mm">Path</th><th>Contents</th></tr></thead>
